<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Hyrcanus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 104 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Hyrcanus I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Hyrcanus was Hasmonean high priest and ruler of Judea from 134 to 104 BCE, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-thassi">Simon Thassi</a>. Taking advantage of Seleucid decline, he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaea">Idumaea</a> and forced its people to adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, and destroyed the Samaritan temple on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>. His reign marked the Hasmonean state's emergence as an independent regional power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus.jpg" alt="John Hyrcanus" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Hyrcanus_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Hyrcanus was born in 164 BCE, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-thassi">Simon Thassi</a> and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judas-maccabeus">Judas Maccabeus</a> and Jonathan Apphus. He was not present at the banquet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> at which his father and two of his brothers were murdered by his brother-in-law, Ptolemy son of Abubus. He took over his father's offices of high priest and ethnarch, but not that of king. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> explains that he was also known as Hyrcanus but gives no reason. Scholars have suggested a family origin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyrcania">Hyrcania</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, a Greek regnal name, or a name given by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> after he campaigned in the region alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-vii-sidetes">Antiochus VII Sidetes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In his first year Antiochus VII marched into Judea and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> for a year. Hyrcanus expelled those who could not help in the defence, then readmitted them at Sukkot and negotiated a truce. Its terms were 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, the dismantling of the walls and Judean service in the war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, and he had to loot the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> to pay. He accompanied Antiochus on the eastern campaign of 130 BCE. After Antiochus was killed in battle against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> in 128 BCE, Hyrcanus stopped paying tribute and raised a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> army.</p><p>Beginning in 113 BCE he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, with his sons Antigonus and Aristobulus in command. The siege lasted a year, the city was devastated and its inhabitants enslaved. He took Medeba after a six-month siege, reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a> to a village and destroyed the Samaritan temple on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, an action dated archaeologically to about 111-110 BCE.</p><p>He conquered Adora and Maresha in Idumea and required the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumeans</a> to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, which some scholars dispute, believing they assimilated peacefully. He rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'s walls, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Baris</a> fortress north of the Temple, and issued coins inscribed &quot;Yohanan the High Priest&quot; and &quot;The Assembly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>&quot;. Two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> decrees established friendship with Judea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hyrcanus died in 104 BCE. By his will the high priesthood passed to his son Judas Aristobulus and civil authority to his widow, but Aristobulus imprisoned his mother and she starved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> reports a quarrel with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a>, after which Hyrcanus sided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>. Rabbinic literature remembers him for enactments, among them an end to writing God's name in ordinary contracts and a limit on work during the intermediate days of festivals. His territorial gains laid the basis for the reigns of his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristobulus-i">Aristobulus I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jannaeus">Alexander Jannaeus</a>, who took the title of king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Hyrcanus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Josephus says that <b>John Hyrcanus</b> I conquered Marisa and Adoraim and made the Idumaeans adopt Jewish law; some scholars have questioned the account since the late 1980s, though most historians still hold that it happened largely as he tells it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/edom">Edom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The temple there stood until the Hasmonean ruler and high priest <b>John Hyrcanus</b> destroyed it and brought Samaria under his control around 120 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/samaritanism">Samaritanism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholarly consensus dates them between the 3rd century BCE and the 1st century CE, and bronze coins found at the same sites run from <b>John Hyrcanus</b>, ruler of Hasmonean Judea from 135 to 104 BCE, to the First Jewish–Roman War of 66 to 73 CE.</blockquote><a href="/work/dead-sea-scrolls">Dead Sea Scrolls · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Hyrcanus</b> conquered Idumaea and destroyed the Samaritan temple on Mount Gerizim; Alexander Jannaeus extended the realm across the Jordan.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
